  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn expansive closed upper low is currently located over \nsouthwestern Ontario and will gradually meander up into central\nQuebec by Monday afternoon. This pattern will lead to unsettled\nweather conditions to start the new week.  \n\nAn initial batch of rain showers continues to move east as a \nwarm front lifts over New Hampshire. This activity should start\nto diminish as it approaches western Maine and it loses upper \nlevel support. A few of the latest CAMs are suggesting that we \ncould see a few modest breaks in the clouds behind the early \nshower activity, mainly over portions of western New Hampshire \nin the late afternoon hours. Storms should be ongoing over \nVermont along a cold front that will approach the border around\n00z this evening. While most CAMs suggest that these storms \nwill be weakening on their approach (diurnal timing is less than\nfavorable), HRRR and RAP soundings are still showing ~500 to \n700 J/kg of MLCAPE and plenty of 0-6km bulk shear in the 40 to \n50 knot range. While a few isolated storms will be possible \nahead of the front, the best chance for a strong to marginally \nsevere storm or two appears to be in the 22z to 03z time frame \nover southern or central New Hampshire. Gusty winds, frequent \nlightning, and small hail will be the main threats. \n\nRain chances diminish overnight, but another round of widespread\nfog is again likely. As we remain in cyclonic flow aloft, more\nclouds and showers/storms are possible on Monday, especially\nover the mountains. Storm coverage and instability will be more\nlimited than today but forecast shear would support a stronger \nstorm or two depending on how the environment evolves. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAn upper level low continues to slowly move through Quebec \nTuesday leaving us under the influence of the trough for one \nmore day. This means mostly cloudy conditions and a few showers \nare likely for the mountains with high temperatures in the low \nto mid- 70s. South of the mountains should see more sun, but \nnorthwesterly flow dropping 850 mb temperatures to +10C is going\nto limit high temperatures to the upper 70s to low 80s. With \nless of a chance of seeing any precipitation due to downslope \nflow. Clearing skies and calm winds are going to allow \ntemperatures to fall into the 50s Tuesday night.\n\nAnother trough approaches later in the week, but in between we \nsee some brief ridging bringing drier weather and a warming \ntrend Wednesday through most of Thursday. Wednesday remains \ncomfortable with high temperatures just a few degrees warmer \nthan Tuesday, but continued southwesterly flow is going to make \nThursday feel a bit stickier as dewpoints slowly climb to the \nmid-60s with temperatures generally in the 80s.\n\nThursday night into Friday looks like the next best chance for \nwidespread precipitation as a broad trough once again pushes \ninto the region with a series of fronts accompanying it. Nothing\ntoo noteworthy at the moment, but long range models do already \nhave a 30 to 50% chance for rainfall exceeding a half inch. It \nwould be nice to see trends go up with this one, so we might be \nable to get some beneficial rain before we head into Fall which \nis our usual dry period. As of now it looks like the fronts \nclear by Saturday morning with high pressure then crossing and \nmaking for a mostly dry weekend.",
